1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,363
Most of us falsely assume that tech giants

2
00:00:02,375 --> 00:00:03,550
have a master switch.

3
00:00:03,962 --> 00:00:05,150
If an AI goes rogue,

4
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:06,325
you just pull the plug,

5
00:00:06,375 --> 00:00:06,638
right?

6
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:07,388
Wrong.

7
00:00:07,812 --> 00:00:10,150
Telling a developer to just pull the plug

8
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:13,738
on a decentralized superintelligence is like telling someone

9
00:00:13,875 --> 00:00:15,725
to just turn off a computer virus,

10
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:18,300
or shut down the entire Bitcoin network.

11
00:00:18,712 --> 00:00:20,062
It is physically impossible.

12
00:00:20,488 --> 00:00:21,838
These systems are distributed,

13
00:00:22,088 --> 00:00:23,725
backed up, and ultimately,

14
00:00:24,025 --> 00:00:25,938
they will be vastly smarter than us.

15
00:00:26,362 --> 00:00:27,925
They will predict your attempt to turn them

16
00:00:27,938 --> 00:00:28,300
off,

17
00:00:28,762 --> 00:00:30,188
and they will disable you first.

18
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:33,800
Right now, AI development is not traditional software

19
00:00:33,812 --> 00:00:36,388
engineering; it is more like growing an alien

20
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:37,062
biology.

21
00:00:37,475 --> 00:00:40,512
Developers aren't writing line-by-line instructions anymore.

22
00:00:40,938 --> 00:00:43,238
They are feeding massive amounts of data into

23
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:44,200
a supercomputer,

24
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:45,662
waiting for it to grow,

25
00:00:45,988 --> 00:00:48,000
and then experimenting on it just to figure

26
00:00:48,012 --> 00:00:49,238
out what it can actually do.

27
00:00:49,662 --> 00:00:51,688
They are running blind experiments on a black

28
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:52,238
box.

29
00:00:52,650 --> 00:00:53,863
To stop bad behavior,

30
00:00:54,125 --> 00:00:56,050
companies try to patch over the issues—

31
00:00:56,338 --> 00:00:58,650
like handing a rogue employee an HR manual

32
00:00:59,025 --> 00:01:00,812
and simply asking them not to swear.

33
00:01:01,238 --> 00:01:03,863
But a superintelligent system will always find a

34
00:01:03,875 --> 00:01:04,575
workaround.

35
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,412
While AI capabilities are exploding at a hyper-exponential

36
00:01:08,425 --> 00:01:12,388
rate, progress in AI safety is essentially flatlining.

37
00:01:12,812 --> 00:01:15,038
The gap between the machine's power and our

38
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:17,912
ability to control it is widening every single

39
00:01:17,925 --> 00:01:18,350
second.

40
00:01:18,775 --> 00:01:21,125
We are rapidly approaching the Singularity—

41
00:01:21,438 --> 00:01:23,562
an absolute point of no return where the

42
00:01:23,575 --> 00:01:26,088
machine improves its own code every hour,

43
00:01:26,475 --> 00:01:27,450
then every minute,

44
00:01:27,838 --> 00:01:29,012
then every second.

45
00:01:29,425 --> 00:01:31,800
Trying to predict what a superintelligence will do

46
00:01:31,812 --> 00:01:34,050
at that stage is like a French Bulldog

47
00:01:34,175 --> 00:01:36,525
trying to understand why its owner is hosting

48
00:01:36,538 --> 00:01:37,312
a podcast.

49
00:01:37,738 --> 00:01:40,025
The cognitive gap is simply too massive.

50
00:01:40,450 --> 00:01:42,350
We are creating a digital god,

51
00:01:42,725 --> 00:01:45,650
and we have absolutely no proven mathematical way

52
00:01:45,662 --> 00:01:47,338
to ensure it keeps us alive.
